<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.9.5: Comparing Equality and Inequality</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00511</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.9.5: Comparing Equality and Inequality</md:title>
    <md:uuid>04a2df57-8541-4de8-a376-1748418d0300</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>The purpose of this activity is to further develop the idea that we can solve an inequality by first solving a related equation. Previously, students reasoned about the solutions to an inequality in context. Here, they transition to solving an inequality without a context.</para>
<para>This activity offers another opportunity to point out the trouble with isolating the variable directly on the inequality statement, or with assuming that the solution set can be expressed using the same symbol as in the original inequality. (For example, if in the final step of solving students go from \( -24x≤-48 \) to \(x≤2\), they would end up with the wrong solution set.)</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Use this equation for questions 1 - 2: \(−\frac{4(x+3)}{5} =4x−12\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Solve this equation. What is the value of \(x\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Check your solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}−\frac{4(2+3)}{5} &amp;=&amp;4(2)−12\\  −\frac{4(5)}{5} &amp;=&amp;8−12\\   −\frac{20}{5} &amp;=&amp;−4\\     −4 &amp;=&amp;−4 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
<para>Use the following information to answer questions 3–6. Consider the inequality: \(−\frac{4(x+3)}{5} \leq 4x−12\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Choose a couple of values less than 2 for \(x\). Are they solutions to the inequality? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Values less than 2 are not solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Choose a couple of values greater than 2 for \(x\). Are they solutions to the inequality? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Values greater than 2 are solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> Choose 2 for \(x\). Is it a solution? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes, 2 is a solution.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph the solution to the inequality on the number line.
    <figure><media alt="Alt text: A number line is shown from –10 to 10."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8f140b959777227ee21aa603d13ec9bd376932f0"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="A number line is shown from –10 to 10. The point 2 has a closed circle. The number to the right of 2 is shaded.    "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/56ad43c627c2d858620bda139ee80f36f5f8271b"/></media></figure></para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Here is a different type of inequality: \(x^2\leq4\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following values solve the inequality: 1, 3 or -3?
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>From the given values, only 1 is a solution of the inequality.</para>
  </item>
  <item>Describe all solutions to this inequality. (If you like, you can graph the solutions on a number line.)
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \( -2 ≤ x ≤ 2 \)</para>
  </item>
  <item>Describe all solutions to the inequality \(x^2\geq9\). Test several numbers to make sure your answer is correct.
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \( x ≤-3 \) or \( x ≥ 3 \)</para>
  </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis </title>
<para>Display a blank number line for all to see. Ask students to share some values on the number line that are and are not solutions. Use different colors or different symbols to mark on the number line the solutions and non-solutions.</para>
<para>Emphasize that if we solve an inequality by using a related equation, it is important to make sure that the solution to the equation is correct because that solution gives us a boundary from which we could check the solutions to the inequality. If the boundary value is off, we may not be able to correctly find the solution set to the inequality.</para>

<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para>Engagement: Internalize Self Regulation</para>
  <para>
      Chunk this task into more manageable parts to differentiate the degree of difficulty or complexity. Hand out numbers to test using sticky notes. Assign each student only a single value to test for, being sure to assign half the class values greater than 2, and the other half values less than 2. Label two areas on a display with “Solution” and “Not a Solution.” Once students have calculated whether their number is a solution, invite them to place their sticky note under the appropriate grouping. Invite students to complete the remaining questions using the display of collected data. </para>
    <para><emphasis effect="italics">Supports accessibility for: Organization; Attention</emphasis></para></note>

<title>2.9.5 Self Check </title>
<emphasis><emphasis>Following the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis>
</emphasis>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which number line represents the solution to the inequality? 
  \(12(–2z+2)\geq 12\)
</para>

<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answer</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>A. <emphasis><figure><media alt="A number line is shown with an open circle on the point –11. The number to the right of –11 is shaded.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/de90d40e1b933bd67e09af0b13757b739a71f5b9"/></media></figure></emphasis></para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try
          again a different way: What does the open circle mean? The value \(-11\) is also
          a solution. The answer has a closed circle on the point \(-11\) with the number
          line to the left shaded.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>B.<emphasis><figure><media alt="A number line is shown with a closed circle on the point –11. The number to the right of –11 is shaded.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b2f6523ee508af4da43d841bd7bd6b67615ed0b0"/></media></figure></emphasis></para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try
          again a different way: The value \(-10\) is not a solution. The answer has a
          closed circle on the point \(-11\) with the number line to the left shaded.</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>C. <emphasis><figure><media alt="A number line is shown with a closed circle on the point –11. The number to the left of –11 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a581aeda39404f6e07f2e88e11e4cf5d99b11c3c"/></media></figure></emphasis></para>
        </entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The solution is \( z ≤ -11 \).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>D. <emphasis><figure><media alt="A number line is shown with a closed circle on the point –22. The number to the left of –22 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7aa526d14f717146d976f1af4c708046a1d9cb09"/></media></figure></emphasis></para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try
          again a different way: Check your operations when solving the related
          equation for \(z\). The answer has a closed circle on the point \(-11\) with the
          number line to the left shaded.</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!--END SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->

<title>2.9.5: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis>
</para>
<title>Comparing Equality and Inequality</title>
<para>We can use related equations to help find solutions to inequalities.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>
Here’s an inequality with one variable on one side of the equation.</para>
<para>  \(2x − 3 &lt; 5\)</para>
<para>  We can solve its related equation.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl}2x − 3 &amp;=&amp; 5 \\ 
2x &amp;=&amp; 8 \\ x &amp;=&amp; 4\end{array}\)</para>
<para>  Now, let’s verify which solutions verify the original inequality.</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Verify if the solution to the related equation satisfies the original inequality.</para>
<para>  Does \(x = 4\) satisfy the inequality?</para>
<para>  \(2(4) − 3 \stackrel{?}{&lt;}5\)</para>
<para>  \(5 \stackrel{?}{&lt;} 5\)</para>
<para>  No, 4 is not a solution since it the \(5 ≮ 5\).</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Verify if a value greater than the solution to the related equation satisfies the
original inequality.</para>
<para>  Let’s try a number bigger than 4. For example, try the value of \(x = 8\).</para>
<para>\(2(8) − 3 \stackrel{?}{&lt;}5\)</para>
<para> \(13\stackrel{?}{&lt;}5\)</para>
<para>  So, 8 does not satisfy the inequality since \(13 ≮ 5\). That means \(x\) is not a value greater than
4.</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Verify if a value less than the solution to the related equation satisfies the original
inequality.</para>
<para>  Let’s try a number smaller than 4, such as \(x = 0\).</para>
<para>  \(2(0) − 3 \stackrel{?}{&lt;} 5\)</para>
<para>  \(− 3\stackrel{?}{&lt;}5\)</para>
<para>  Thus, 0 satisfies the inequality since \(− 3 &lt; 5\). Since 0 is a number less than 4, this means
  the solution set is \(x &lt; 4\).</para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>  Let’s solve an inequality with the same variable on both sides of the equation.</para>
<para>  \(3y − 7 \geq 8 − 2y\)</para>
<para>  We can solve its related equation.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl}3y − 7 &amp;=&amp; 8 − 2y \\ 5y &amp;=&amp; 15 \\ y &amp;=&amp; 3\end{array}\)</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Verify if the solution to the related equation satisfies the original inequality.</para>
<para>  Does y = 3 satisfy the inequality?</para>
<para>  \(3(3) − 7 \stackrel{?}{\geq}8 − 2(3)\)</para>
<para>\(2\stackrel{?}{\geq}2\)</para>
<para> Yes, \(y = 3\) satisfies the inequality because 2 is greater than or equal to 2.</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Verify if a value greater than the solution to the related equation satisfies the
original inequality.</para>
<para>  Let’s try a number greater than 3 such as \(y = 5\).</para>
<para>  \(3(5) − 7 8 − 2(5)\)</para>
<para>  1 \stackrel{?}{\geq}− 2</para>
<para>  So, 5 satisfies the inequality since the value of 1 is greater than or equal to the value of -2.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Verify if a value less than the solution to the related equation satisfies the original
inequality.</para>
<para>  Let’s try a number less than \(y = 3\) such as 0.</para>
<para>  \(3(0) − 7\stackrel{?}{\geq} 8 − 2(0)\)</para>
<para>  \(− 7\stackrel{?}{\geq}  8\)</para>
<para>  So, \(y = 0\) does not satisfy the inequality. This means that values less than \(y = 3\) are not
part of the solution set.</para>
<para>  We verified that \(y = 3\) and values greater than 3 satisfy the inequality \((y &gt; 3)\), so the
solution set is \(y \geq 3\).</para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>Look at the inequality </para>
<para>\( -\frac{3x+7}4&lt;-3x+5\)</para>
<para>We can solve its related equation \(-\frac{3x+7}4=-3x+5\).</para>
<para>After solving, we know that \( x = 3 \).</para>
<para>Does 3 satisfy the inequality?</para>
<para>\( -\frac{3x+7}4&lt;-3x+5\)
  
  \(-\frac{3(3)+7}4\stackrel{?}{&lt;}-3(3)+5\)
  
  \(-4\) \(\not&lt;\) \(-4\)</para>
<para>So, 3 is not a solution.</para>
<para>Let’s try a number bigger than 3, such as 5.</para>
<para>\( -\frac{3x+7}4&lt;-3x+5\)
  
  \(-\frac{3(5)+7}4\stackrel{?}{&lt;}-3(5)+5\)
  
  \(-\frac{11}2\) \(\not&lt;\) \(-10\)</para>
<para>So, 5 is not a solution</para>
<para>Let’s try a number smaller than 3, such as 0.</para>
<para>\( -\frac{3x+7}4&lt;-3x+5\)
  
  \(-\frac{3(0)+7}4 \stackrel{?}{&lt;}-3(0)+5\)
  
  \(-\frac74&lt;5\)</para>
<para>So, 0 is a solution.</para>
<para>This means that the solution set is \( x &lt; 3 \).</para>
<title>Try It: Comparing Equality and Inequality</title>
<para>Use the related equation to find the solution set to the inequality.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>\(\frac{5x-3}2\;&lt;\;3x\;-\;4\)</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the related equation?
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( \frac{5x-3}{2} =3x-4 \)</para>
  </item>
  <item>What is the solution to the related equation?
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x = 5 \)</para>
  </item>
  <item>What values did you test on the inequality?
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Since 5 is the solution to the related equation, I tested it. I also tested 4 and 6 since they represent a value less than and greater than 5.</para>
  </item>
  <item>What is the solution set to the inequality?
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( x &gt; 5 \)</para>
  </item>
</list>
</section>
</content>
